          {
            "line": 73,
            "context": [
              "",
              "A poll showed that Bardot was the main topic of conversation in 47% of all households in the France of 1960. However, her popularity was uneven in France. Women were known to spit on her image and priests denounced her from the pulpit. She was the femme fatale exemplar. The press chronicled her misfortunes with relish, her marriage misadventures, the abandonment of her baby son and her suicide attempts. In a celebrated essay, Simone de Beauvoir diagnosed the threat she posed as a force of nature, \"a shameless hussy.\"",
              ""
            ]
          },
          {
            "line": 83,
            "context": [
              "",
              "After she and Charrier divorced, the latter gained sole custody of their son Nicolas. When Nicolas was 12, he asked Bardot if he could stay with her, but she turned him away in favour of party guests. Nicolas was hurt and did not speak to her afterwards. In her memoirs, Bardot wrote that she loved Nicolas \"the most in the world\", but Nicolas wanted nothing to do with her. When he married Norwegian model Anne-Line Bjerkan in 1984, Bardot was not invited to the wedding. Bardot became a grandmother when the two had daughters Anna-Camilla and Théa-Joséphine in 1985 and 1990. She tried to make peace with him on multiple occasions, but to no avail. In 1997, Charrier and Nicolas sued her and her publisher, Grasset, for the hurtful remarks she had made in her memoir. She was ordered to pay Charrier £17,000 and Nicolas £11,000. In 2018, she stated that she and Nicolas, now a grandfather himself, were on good terms, speaking regularly and visiting each other once a year.",
              ""
            ]
          },
